\subsection{Deployed reduced-MSE schedule and coefficient meaning}
\label{sec:riccati-similarity-design-guidance}
\label{sec:riccati-deployed-cadence}

The reduced physical-MSE calculation of
Sec.~\ref{sec:adaptation-reduced-mse-envelope}, together with the spectral
coefficient evaluation of Sec.~\ref{sec:adaptation-reduced-mse-validation},
sets the deployed OU--III regularizer target.  For the realized target cadence
\begin{equation}
T_{S,\star}=\clip(c_T\tau_\star,T_{S,\min},T_{S,\max}),
\label{eq:engineering-pseudo-cadence}
\end{equation}
the SpectralMSE target is
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
r_{S,\star}^{\mathrm{MSE}}
=C_J q_{\mathrm{eff}}^{1/14}
 \widehat\sigma_{a,B,\star}^{\,6/7}
 \tau_\star^{24/7}T_{S,\star}^{-1/2},
\qquad C_J=0.0538.}
\label{eq:adapt-default-rs-law}
\end{equation}
Here the acceleration amplitude is the physical band-limited wave statistic,
not the OU-prior standard deviation.  Under the strong-observation reduction
used to interpret the coefficient, $q_{\mathrm{eff}}\simeq2R_a h$ to leading
order.  The exponent $1/14$ makes the optimum only weakly sensitive to the
low-frequency acceleration-error intensity, whereas the $3/7$ dependence on
the displacement spectral moment gives the true wave content substantially
more leverage over the optimum regularization corner.

The dimensionless coefficient $C_J$ collects the normalized spectral moment of
the self-similar wave family and the numerical constants of the reduced
optimization.  It is therefore obtained from the measured/reference spectral
shape rather than from a comparison with an earlier regularizer schedule.  The
record-wise spectral calculation gives the default $C_J=0.0538$ used by the
implementation; complete-filter validation then tests the resulting estimator.

The factor $T_S^{-1/2}$ is already part of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:adapt-default-rs-law}.  Away from cadence clamps,
$T_S\propto\tau$ and
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
r_{S,\star}^{\mathrm{MSE}}
\propto\widehat\sigma_{a,B}^{\,6/7}\tau^{41/14}.}
\label{eq:adapt-mse-unclipped-scaling}
\end{equation}
Thus the deployed period exponent is $41/14\simeq2.929$ and the deployed
acceleration exponent is $6/7\simeq0.857$.

The resulting current schedule is
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\tau_\star&=c_\tau T_{\rm sea},\\
\sigma_{aw,\star}&=c_\sigma\widehat\sigma_{a,B},\\
r_{S,\star}&=C_J q_{\mathrm{eff}}^{1/14}
 \widehat\sigma_{a,B}^{\,6/7}\tau_\star^{24/7}T_{S,\star}^{-1/2}.
\end{aligned}}
\label{eq:adapt-three-layer-summary}
\end{equation}
The first two mappings set the approximate OU prior.  The third independently
sets the low-frequency regularizer by balancing drift rejection against
distortion of genuine displacement.

\subsection{Dimensional context for the integral state}
\label{sec:adaptation-dimensional-context}

The third-integral coordinate still has a useful dimensional reference.  If an
acceleration scale is chosen as $\sigma_{aw}$, the corresponding self-similar
coordinate scale can be written
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
r_{S,\mathrm{dim}}=C_R\sigma_{aw}\tau^3.}
\label{eq:adapt-rs-base}
\end{equation}
This is a coordinate-scale statement only.  It is not the deployed
pseudo-measurement schedule and does not determine the displacement-MSE optimum.
Combining this dimensional scale with the reduced regularization-corner relation
would give
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
(\omega_R\tau)^6
=\frac{q_{\mathrm{eff}}}
 {C_R^2\sigma_{aw}^2T_S}.}
\label{eq:adapt-cR-spectral-meaning}
\end{equation}
The equation is retained only to distinguish dimensional similarity from the
optimization that leads to Eq.~\eqref{eq:adapt-default-rs-law}; no numerical
comparison with a historical cubic schedule is used in the present design
argument.
